#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069928 :tutorial/content "The **limit** of a function is the value that the function approaches. For example, the function $y=f(x)$ shown below is not defined at $x=2,$ as represented by the open circle there $-$ but when we *approach* $x=2$, we see that $f(x)$ *approaches* the value $y=1.$\n\n![](images/f7/f7081d649c6cdfa0a19a0fb2de93b97ee700ad922f34530c7f3e5c2a3228c9fb.png)\n\nAs $x$ approaches $2$, the value of $f(x)$ approaches $1.$ In other words, the limit of $f(x),$ as $x$ approaches $2,$ is $1$. We can express this mathematically as \n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 2}f(x)=1 \\, .\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e52528a2-ed6a-5d07-89d4-1a74d70eb6b9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 138 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069924 :tutorial/content "The limit of a function at some point has **nothing to do** with the value of the function at that point.\n\nFor example, the following functions $g(x)$ and $h(x)$ have *different values* at $x=2,$ but they have the *same limit* at $x=2.$\n\n![](images/6a/6a73b74072a8a990c91a38553d519f4913370d34ef0251b7e45cc664da40517d.png)\n\n![](images/06/06e150aacae774b4fe883a2750ef13cebbddf2ea871e5a682f2587c47a54615a.png)\n\nThe actual values of $g(2)$ and $h(2)$ are irrelevant for determining the limit at $x=2$. All that matters is what values the functions *look like* they are approaching as $x$ approaches $2$. \n\nNear $x=2,$ both $g(x)$ and $h(x)$ appear to be approaching $y=1$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 2}g(x)=1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 2}h(x)=1 \\, .\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "ea300af0-adfe-5df2-8b4d-ae23430245a7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1333 :tutorial/title "Limit Has Nothing to Do With the Function Value"}]}
